{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if a greater proportion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher values) relative to unfavorable ones (zero). In this case, both lotteries have a non-zero outcome (2400 or 2500 Euros) and a zero outcome. The favorable outcome is the non-zero amount, and the unfavorable is zero. To assess skewness: a lottery is more left-skewed if the probability mass is concentrated on the higher outcome (favorable) rather than the zero outcome. Lottery A has a 33% chance of 2500 Euros and 67% chance of 0. Lottery B has a 34% chance of 2400 Euros and 66% chance of 0. Thus, A has a higher probability (33% vs. 34%) on the favorable outcome (2500 > 2400), and a lower probability (67% vs. 66%) on the zero outcome. Therefore, A is more left-skewed than B. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which in this case is A.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'A, da ich 100€ mehr gewinnen könnte, und es nur ein Prozent weniger Gewinnchance wäre als zu B.' This translates to: 'A, because I could win 100€ more, and the chance of winning is only 1% less than with B.' The individual considers the higher payoff (2500 vs. 2400) in lottery A and notes that the probability of winning is only 1% lower (33% vs. 34%). The individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution, or any measure of asymmetry in the outcomes. The justification is based solely on the absolute difference in the amount won and a small difference in probability. No reference is made to the shape of the distribution or to left-skewness. The deliberation focuses on the magnitude of the gain and the minor drop in probability, not on the distributional asymmetry of outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual evaluates the difference in expected value and the small probability difference, not the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. The decision reason requires assessing whether one lottery has a greater concentration of probability on the favorable outcome (i.e., higher value), which would indicate greater left-skewness. The individual does not mention or reason about the shape of the probability distribution. Instead, the justification is based on a simple comparison of payoff and probability difference, not on skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process. The individual's reasoning is inconsistent with the skewness-based rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
